[Unit]
Description=Sofia PT archive retention (drop day files and static snapshots older than the publication window)

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# The first thing on this box that destroys data, so the window is derived,
# not picked. The VPS copy is the only one that survives the laptop being
# gone, until a day reaches a published Zenodo dataset version — so the
# window must never be shorter than the gap between those versions. The
# cadence is quarterly until v2 (CLAUDE.md section 3, [F]), hence 90 days.
# When versions go monthly, 45 is enough and takes the disk from ~71% to
# ~54%; revisit then rather than trimming for its own sake.
#
# Measured on the live box 2026-09-05: 8.7 GB disk, 2.7 GB of it system,
# growth 34 MB/day (16.3 MB/day for gzipped day files averaged over a week,
# 18 MB/day for static snapshots — the agency changes the feed daily, five
# snapshots in five days) plus a standing ~430 MB in the three uncompressed
# days sofia-compress.service has not reached yet. Unpruned, that hits the
# 80% DigitalOcean alert in 108 days and fills the disk in 159. At 90 days
# the steady state is ~6.2 GB (71%), which leaves the alert meaning
# "something is wrong" instead of "the calendar advanced".
#
# What the name tests select, and why each one is written this way:
#   ????-??-??.jsonl[.gz]  the day files, the bulk of the archive. Both forms
#                          because a 90-day-old uncompressed day means
#                          sofia-compress.service is broken, and this should
#                          still reclaim it. gzip keeps the original mtime,
#                          so a compressed day is still aged from its own
#                          date, not from the night it was compressed.
#   gtfs_*.zip             static snapshots, 18 MB each and half the growth.
# Deliberately NOT matched: *.polls.jsonl[.gz] (the date glob is exact, so
# the heartbeat companions fall outside it) and *.manifest.json. The
# heartbeats cost ~15 MB/year and hold the poll-by-poll trace the manifests
# only summarise; the manifests are kilobytes and live in git.
#
# Age-based and server-side on purpose. Deleting only what fetch_data.sh has
# verified against the laptop would be safer on paper, but that check runs
# from the laptop, and the disk fills precisely in the stretches when the
# laptop is off — which is the case this timer exists for.
#
# -mtime +90 means at least 91 whole days: GNU find truncates the age to
# whole days, the same off-by-a-day-in-the-safe-direction as
# sofia-compress.service.
ExecStart=/usr/bin/find /opt/sofia-pt/data -type f ( -name '????-??-??.jsonl' -o -name '????-??-??.jsonl.gz' -o -name 'gtfs_*.zip' ) -mtime +90 -delete
User=root
